INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'knowledge_agent' with arguments: {"source_title": "DOJ indictment and Senate Volume II / platform analysis", "source_url": "SRC-001; SRC-003; SRC-004; SRC-002", "text": "Evidence returned by benchmark tools: DOJ indictment alleges the Russian Internet Research Agency (IRA) operated from at least 2014 to interfere with U.S. political/electoral processes including 2016; it used false U.S. personas, stolen identities, social-media pages/groups posing as U.S. activists, U.S.-based infrastructure, paid ads, rallies, and unwitting Americans. It states a strategic goal of sowing discord, and by early/mid-2016 supporting Donald Trump and disparaging Hillary Clinton. It describes a translator project on YouTube, Facebook, Instagram, and Twitter, more than 80 employees by July 2016, millions-dollar annual budget, and a stated goal of spreading distrust toward candidates and the political system. Concord Management and Consulting/Concord Catering are alleged as primary funders; Project Lakhta monthly budget exceeded 73 million rubles (over $1.25m) in September 2016, with money disguised as software support and routed through affiliates; Yevgeniy Prigozhin controlled Concord and approved/supported operations (all are indictment allegations, not convictions in the extracted text). Senate Volume II reports that Russian operatives associated with the St. Petersburg IRA conducted an information-warfare campaign in 2016 using targeted ads, falsified news, self-generated content, and platform tools, attempting to deceive tens of millions, polarize Americans by societal/ideological/racial differences, provoke real-world events, and covertly support Russia's favored candidate. Senate findings: IRA sought to harm Clinton and support Trump at Kremlin direction; broader aim was discord and distrust; African-Americans were the most targeted group; over 66% of Facebook ads contained race-related terms; Blacktivist generated 11.2m Facebook engagements; paid ads were not key compared with organic content; about $100,000 ad spend over two years versus approx. $1.25m monthly operational costs; nearly 3,400 Facebook/Instagram ads, 61,500 Facebook posts, 116,000 Instagram posts, and 10.4m tweets. It also found offline cooptation of unwitting Americans and requests for Trump Campaign assistance. Platform analysis SRC-004 reports tens of millions reached, over 30m users shared IRA Facebook/Instagram posts 2015-17, multi-platform evolution from Twitter in 2013, organic activity more far-reaching than ads, campaign tactics including African-American voter boycott/wrong-procedure messaging, extreme-right confrontation, and sensationalist/conspiratorial misinformation across the spectrum. NPR SRC-002 corroborates race emphasis and post-election increases: Instagram +238%, Facebook +59%, Twitter +52%, YouTube citations +84%."}
